WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:07.470
&gt;&gt; Anytime Waterloo
reminiscing [inaudible].

00:00:07.471 --> 00:00:09.075
&gt;&gt; So, thank you all for coming.

00:00:09.076 --> 00:00:12.194
It's really a pleasure to

00:00:12.195 --> 00:00:15.910
be hosting Dr. Jesse Hoey here
at Microsoft Research, Montreal.

00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:18.320
So, Dr. Hoey is as
some of you may know,

00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:20.340
an associate professor in
the David Cheriton School

00:00:20.341 --> 00:00:22.440
of Computer Science at
University of Waterloo,

00:00:22.441 --> 00:00:25.725
where he leads the computational
human intelligence laboratory.

00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:28.880
He holds a PhD from
the University British Columbia,

00:00:28.881 --> 00:00:32.165
has published over a 100
peer-reviewed scientific papers and

00:00:32.166 --> 00:00:34.310
has received a number of awards

00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:37.685
including from Microsoft,
have to talk about that one.

00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:41.635
The AAI and the International
Association for pattern recognition.

00:00:41.636 --> 00:00:44.090
He's also for the American
Sociological Association.

00:00:44.091 --> 00:00:46.190
I think he maybe one of
the only computer scientists

00:00:46.191 --> 00:00:49.190
that I know of who
has two articles in

00:00:49.191 --> 00:00:51.020
the American Sociological
Review which is

00:00:51.021 --> 00:00:56.110
the best sociological review
in states possibly the world.

00:00:56.111 --> 00:00:58.350
So, he's very interdisciplinary.

00:00:58.351 --> 00:01:00.620
He's an area chair for the
International Joint conference on

00:01:00.621 --> 00:01:02.810
our [inaudible]
Artificial Intelligence and

00:01:02.811 --> 00:01:04.130
was the program chair and

00:01:04.131 --> 00:01:07.915
the 10th EAI International
Conference and Pervasive Computing.

00:01:07.916 --> 00:01:09.845
He'll be speaking today on

00:01:09.846 --> 00:01:11.465
social and emotional
Artificial Intelligence.

00:01:11.466 --> 00:01:13.100
So, please welcome Dr. Hoey.

00:01:13.101 --> 00:01:17.450
&gt;&gt; All right. Thanks, Luke

00:01:17.451 --> 00:01:21.560
and thanks for inviting me here
and hosting me this visit.

00:01:21.561 --> 00:01:23.150
So, Montreal is my hometown.

00:01:23.151 --> 00:01:25.130
So, it's always nice
to come back here

00:01:25.131 --> 00:01:28.120
and see how things have
changed a little bit.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:32.330
So, I'm going to talk to you
about Artificial Intelligence,

00:01:32.331 --> 00:01:35.750
but my perspective on it might
be a little bit different

00:01:35.751 --> 00:01:39.890
than the mainstream view
of AI these days.

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.930
But I'll start with this slide with

00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:46.350
some pictures of
some big achievements in AI.

00:01:46.351 --> 00:01:49.640
So, obviously Artificial
Intelligence has come a long

00:01:49.641 --> 00:01:54.350
way from computers they
could be chess grandmasters.

00:01:54.351 --> 00:01:57.560
This is quite a while ago now to

00:01:57.561 --> 00:01:59.720
self-driving cars and computers that

00:01:59.721 --> 00:02:02.450
can play much more challenging
games nowadays.

00:02:02.451 --> 00:02:05.810
But, one of the things if we look at

00:02:05.811 --> 00:02:09.560
Artificial Intelligence
in group behavior,

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:12.080
then the situation looks
a little bit different.

00:02:12.081 --> 00:02:17.000
So, this is a picture from
the 2017 robot cup which

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:24.485
is probably the quintessential
multi-agent problem.

00:02:24.486 --> 00:02:28.500
These robots are playing on a team,

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:31.910
so it is funny because
they're robots and they're

00:02:31.911 --> 00:02:33.920
a little bit difficult to

00:02:33.921 --> 00:02:36.650
maintain a standing
and things like that.

00:02:36.651 --> 00:02:39.260
But, they do know that
they're playing on

00:02:39.261 --> 00:02:42.530
a team and they probably have
some centralized controller,

00:02:42.531 --> 00:02:45.080
but they really have
no notion of what

00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:48.295
being a team member means, right?

00:02:48.296 --> 00:02:51.480
What essentially they lack is

00:02:51.481 --> 00:02:56.720
any idea of empathy or culture
or emotion or altruism.

00:02:56.721 --> 00:03:00.830
So, they don't have a way of
interpreting what other agents or how

00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:05.000
other agents are handling
the situation as team members,

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:07.015
as a social group.

00:03:07.016 --> 00:03:10.600
There has been some efforts
to make this happen.

00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:14.320
So, let's try to put some emotional
behaviors into our robots.

00:03:14.321 --> 00:03:18.650
So, here's this guy who's happy
and now he's not so happy,

00:03:18.651 --> 00:03:21.120
maybe he'd let in a goal
or something like that.

00:03:21.121 --> 00:03:24.235
But, of course, these are
just purely scripted behaviors.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:28.390
This robot has no idea what
this means to any other robot.

00:03:28.391 --> 00:03:29.950
In fact, no other robot has

00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:32.805
any other way of interpreting
these signals, okay?

00:03:32.806 --> 00:03:36.640
If you look at humans when
they play games together,

00:03:36.641 --> 00:03:39.520
you see this vast array
of emotions going on.

00:03:39.521 --> 00:03:41.830
So, this is a short clip
from the- this is

00:03:41.831 --> 00:03:46.040
the bursa junior team that
just beat this Korean team.

00:03:46.041 --> 00:03:49.190
You can see this incredible array
of emotions going on and

00:03:49.191 --> 00:03:52.930
different cultural signals that
they're sending to each other.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:56.905
Like this guy is using
the face slap which must be

00:03:56.906 --> 00:04:03.305
some Spanish expression of empathy.

00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:10.200
I'm not sure it works so well with
this Korean guy there is kind-.

00:04:10.201 --> 00:04:13.310
So anyways, the bottom line is
that Artificial Intelligence has

00:04:13.311 --> 00:04:16.730
come a long way and we can do
some remarkable things with it,

00:04:16.731 --> 00:04:21.020
but when we get to the point
where AI or robots or

00:04:21.021 --> 00:04:22.700
Artificial Intelligence
systems are trying to

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:25.610
interact with humans or
with groups of humans,

00:04:25.611 --> 00:04:29.190
the situation looks quite different.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.020
So, this is a well-known problem.

00:04:33.021 --> 00:04:35.705
If you look at self-driving
cars, for example,

00:04:35.706 --> 00:04:39.710
self-driving cars
have some difficulty

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:42.140
when they get to busy intersections,

00:04:42.141 --> 00:04:44.000
for example, and one
of the reasons that

00:04:44.001 --> 00:04:45.950
they have difficulty is that they

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:50.450
can't display enough aggression
to get through the intersection.

00:04:50.451 --> 00:04:52.595
So, this cartoon by the oatmeal

00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:54.895
is meant to sort of
lampoon that a little bit.

00:04:54.896 --> 00:04:57.860
This is a situation where
this Google car is trying to

00:04:57.861 --> 00:05:00.935
negotiate passage with this human
through an intersection.

00:05:00.936 --> 00:05:03.170
You've probably all have
been in this situation where

00:05:03.171 --> 00:05:05.555
you don't know who's
going to go next and

00:05:05.556 --> 00:05:09.320
somebody's going to take
the bull by the horns and drive

00:05:09.321 --> 00:05:11.480
forward and that aggression level

00:05:11.481 --> 00:05:13.490
is a culturally determined thing.

00:05:13.491 --> 00:05:14.840
So, if you go to different cultures,

00:05:14.841 --> 00:05:16.895
people have different ways
of doing this.

00:05:16.896 --> 00:05:18.469
So, in this situation,

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.750
the humans like making this way too

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:24.650
complicated and the robot has
no idea of what's going on.

00:05:24.651 --> 00:05:27.110
The bottom line is that these robots,

00:05:27.111 --> 00:05:31.495
these self-driving cars have
no model of how a human works.

00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:33.590
Okay. So, they have
no human model in them.

00:05:33.591 --> 00:05:36.350
They have models of how
things move in the world,

00:05:36.351 --> 00:05:38.420
but they don't have
a model of something

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:39.530
like the level of

00:05:39.531 --> 00:05:42.565
aggression that's needed to
get through an intersection.

00:05:42.566 --> 00:05:46.820
So, Artificial Intelligence
has taken a stab

00:05:46.821 --> 00:05:51.725
at putting emotions into
Artificial Intelligence Systems.

00:05:51.726 --> 00:05:55.010
This has coined this term

00:05:55.011 --> 00:05:57.815
that's used to describe this
as Affective Computing.

00:05:57.816 --> 00:05:58.970
So, here I have a picture of,

00:05:58.971 --> 00:06:01.970
I don't know why put Von Neumann
there but anyways there he is.

00:06:01.971 --> 00:06:04.130
He didn't work on emotions so much,

00:06:04.131 --> 00:06:08.180
but Herb Simon was probably
the first person to think

00:06:08.181 --> 00:06:13.399
seriously about how could you
put emotions into a machine,

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.530
what would that mean?

00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:18.080
He basically described
emotions as being

00:06:18.081 --> 00:06:21.160
something that disrupts cognition.

00:06:21.161 --> 00:06:23.810
So, we're thinking
all these high-level thoughts

00:06:23.811 --> 00:06:25.370
and we're reasoning about what to do

00:06:25.371 --> 00:06:29.585
next and then emotions come
in and disrupt everything.

00:06:29.586 --> 00:06:32.990
But the disruption is
there for a reason.

00:06:32.991 --> 00:06:35.030
Enables us to better

00:06:35.031 --> 00:06:38.005
coordinate with other
human beings, for example.

00:06:38.006 --> 00:06:41.960
Then Rose Picard wrote the book,

00:06:41.961 --> 00:06:43.790
I guess wrote the book
on Affective Computing

00:06:43.791 --> 00:06:48.715
1997 and the book
starts with this quote,

00:06:48.716 --> 00:06:50.390
"This book proposes that we give

00:06:50.391 --> 00:06:52.670
computers the ability to recognize,

00:06:52.671 --> 00:06:56.210
express and in some cases
have emotions,

00:06:56.211 --> 00:06:58.060
is this not absurd?"

00:06:58.061 --> 00:07:02.540
So, in 1997, nobody had
really thought this

00:07:02.541 --> 00:07:04.880
before written it down and it did

00:07:04.881 --> 00:07:07.550
seem very absurd but she
was a professor at MIT.

00:07:07.551 --> 00:07:11.150
So, she could get away with
saying absurd things I guess.

00:07:11.151 --> 00:07:14.600
Since then the field has
grown and we're just now and

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:16.820
IEEE Transactions on
Affective Computing

00:07:16.821 --> 00:07:18.830
and there's a couple
of major conferences.

00:07:18.831 --> 00:07:22.990
So, there's a fair bit
of work in the area.

00:07:22.991 --> 00:07:29.050
Rose Picard gave a talk
at AAAI in 2017,

00:07:29.051 --> 00:07:33.290
describing some of her
amazing work on using wearables

00:07:33.291 --> 00:07:38.205
to detect emotions for
healthcare applications.

00:07:38.206 --> 00:07:42.370
They've really done
some amazing things in doing that.

00:07:42.371 --> 00:07:45.090
But if you look at
Artificial Intelligence,

00:07:45.091 --> 00:07:48.355
so for example, in the 2017 AAAI,

00:07:48.356 --> 00:07:50.990
I looked through all of
the titles when she was giving

00:07:50.991 --> 00:07:54.925
this talk and found the word
emotion appeared three times.

00:07:54.926 --> 00:07:58.260
That's three times in
about a thousand papers.

00:07:58.261 --> 00:08:00.885
The same thing in 2018.

00:08:00.886 --> 00:08:05.060
So, I feel like the study
of emotions has been pushed

00:08:05.061 --> 00:08:07.040
a little bit to the side by a lot

00:08:07.041 --> 00:08:09.745
of the recent developments in AI.

00:08:09.746 --> 00:08:12.445
But it'll make a comeback.

00:08:12.446 --> 00:08:15.590
Okay. So, I think that the view that

00:08:15.591 --> 00:08:18.815
Herb Simon proposed which
has sort of been largely,

00:08:18.816 --> 00:08:23.045
what a lot of effective
computing has worked from,

00:08:23.046 --> 00:08:26.945
that emotions are
sort of disrupters of cognition.

00:08:26.946 --> 00:08:32.375
I think that this may actually
be fundamentally backwards.

00:08:32.376 --> 00:08:34.040
I'll try to explain why,

00:08:34.041 --> 00:08:36.680
and then I'm going to give in
this talk a model that I think

00:08:36.681 --> 00:08:39.380
puts the cart before the sorry,

00:08:39.381 --> 00:08:40.640
puts the horse before the cart

00:08:40.641 --> 00:08:42.965
instead of the cart before the horse.

00:08:42.966 --> 00:08:45.665
So, I try to motivate it with

00:08:45.666 --> 00:08:49.325
some evidence that we have
from neurophysiology.

00:08:49.326 --> 00:08:52.340
So, this is a very
highly stylized picture

00:08:52.341 --> 00:08:54.515
of what's going on
in the human brain,

00:08:54.516 --> 00:08:57.485
and from this paper
by Zhu and Thagard.

00:08:57.486 --> 00:08:59.314
What they do is, they describe

00:08:59.315 --> 00:09:03.215
two different systems that
happen in human cognition.

00:09:03.216 --> 00:09:06.350
They call it a low
road and high road.

00:09:06.351 --> 00:09:08.480
The low road is something that is

00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:10.490
these are both cognitive processes.

00:09:10.491 --> 00:09:12.590
So, these are not instinctive things.

00:09:12.591 --> 00:09:16.475
These are actually things
that humans reason about but,

00:09:16.476 --> 00:09:19.235
the low road is something that takes,

00:09:19.236 --> 00:09:22.595
avoids the large parts
of the sensory cortex

00:09:22.596 --> 00:09:26.464
and is something that
happens very very quickly,

00:09:26.465 --> 00:09:31.910
and is able to give human beings
a rough idea of what to do next.

00:09:31.911 --> 00:09:34.730
Okay. Then the high road

00:09:34.731 --> 00:09:38.330
is a pathway through
the brain that takes longer,

00:09:38.331 --> 00:09:42.470
but then it goes through
these cortical reasoning aspects,

00:09:42.471 --> 00:09:44.990
and that's where you do
your actual cognitions and

00:09:44.991 --> 00:09:48.380
thinking about how the world
works, and so on and so forth.

00:09:48.381 --> 00:09:52.130
The interplay between these
two systems is really

00:09:52.131 --> 00:09:53.690
quite an interesting part of how

00:09:53.691 --> 00:09:57.650
humans make decisions
and act in the world.

00:09:57.651 --> 00:10:02.360
With again this low road
providing this very fast, quick,

00:10:02.361 --> 00:10:04.100
and dirty solutions to things,

00:10:04.101 --> 00:10:06.770
and this high road coming in later if

00:10:06.771 --> 00:10:10.205
necessary and trying
to fix things up.

00:10:10.206 --> 00:10:15.320
Right. You see the same,

00:10:15.321 --> 00:10:18.680
the same idea of two systems coming

00:10:18.681 --> 00:10:21.920
out from psychology and
behavioral economics.

00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:24.470
So, perhaps the most famous example

00:10:24.471 --> 00:10:27.110
of this is this book
by Daniel Kahneman;

00:10:27.111 --> 00:10:28.730
Thinking Fast and Slow,

00:10:28.731 --> 00:10:30.890
where he describes these
two systems again.

00:10:30.891 --> 00:10:35.344
The Fast System 1 which operates
automatically and quickly,

00:10:35.345 --> 00:10:38.030
and then the Slow System 2 which is

00:10:38.031 --> 00:10:41.585
Effortful Mental Activity
and Complex Calculations.

00:10:41.586 --> 00:10:44.660
The way that they work together in

00:10:44.661 --> 00:10:48.845
Kahneman's work or what he's
just how he describes it,

00:10:48.846 --> 00:10:51.620
is that this System
1 is the thing it is

00:10:51.621 --> 00:10:54.935
always in operation that
humans are using to get by.

00:10:54.936 --> 00:10:56.765
Then when that runs into trouble,

00:10:56.766 --> 00:10:58.670
people call this System 2 into

00:10:58.671 --> 00:11:00.995
play in order to help them
figure out what to do.

00:11:00.996 --> 00:11:03.560
Right. But people do not want to go

00:11:03.561 --> 00:11:06.590
and use this System 2 because
it takes more energy.

00:11:06.591 --> 00:11:09.320
If you can avoid using
it, might as well.

00:11:09.321 --> 00:11:13.500
You get by with much less
if you can go with this.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.100
You see the same idea throughout

00:11:16.101 --> 00:11:19.370
a vast array of
different disciplines.

00:11:19.371 --> 00:11:22.070
People running into
the same concept of

00:11:22.071 --> 00:11:25.430
these two systems in
the brain. All right.

00:11:25.431 --> 00:11:27.590
So, this is what I see as

00:11:27.591 --> 00:11:32.390
the traditional view of emotion
in Artificial Intelligence,

00:11:32.391 --> 00:11:35.270
which essentially is what
Herb Simon was talking about.

00:11:35.271 --> 00:11:37.205
So, you have some of the environment,

00:11:37.206 --> 00:11:38.945
you perceive what's going on,

00:11:38.946 --> 00:11:41.240
then you have your
cognitive systems that are

00:11:41.241 --> 00:11:44.570
reasoning about the environment,
what's happening.

00:11:44.571 --> 00:11:47.300
So, you're playing chess and
you're figuring out where

00:11:47.301 --> 00:11:48.725
all the chess pieces are and

00:11:48.726 --> 00:11:50.465
how you're going to
move them, and so on.

00:11:50.466 --> 00:11:54.260
Then that drives you
to take some action.

00:11:54.261 --> 00:11:56.540
Then your emotions or
things that are happening

00:11:56.541 --> 00:11:58.880
kind of on the side after the fact.

00:11:58.881 --> 00:12:01.895
So, after you sorted out everything
that's going on in the world,

00:12:01.896 --> 00:12:04.595
then you figure out what emotion
you should be feeling,

00:12:04.596 --> 00:12:08.495
and then you have that emotion
and that can modify

00:12:08.496 --> 00:12:13.025
how you're thinking about
the tasks that you're doing.

00:12:13.026 --> 00:12:17.570
So, really a cognitive machine
disrupted by by emotion.

00:12:17.571 --> 00:12:22.010
Again these disruptions that
the emotion causes may be useful.

00:12:22.011 --> 00:12:23.990
Right. They may be
actually pointing you in

00:12:23.991 --> 00:12:27.035
a direction that may be useful to go,

00:12:27.036 --> 00:12:28.835
but really they are,

00:12:28.836 --> 00:12:31.025
take second place to this,

00:12:31.026 --> 00:12:34.770
this higher-level cognitive
reasoning engine.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.885
The theory that I'm
going to describe today

00:12:37.886 --> 00:12:40.120
really reverses these two systems,

00:12:40.121 --> 00:12:42.340
and I think puts the affect of

00:12:42.341 --> 00:12:44.440
processing system as the thing that

00:12:44.441 --> 00:12:46.980
has the first kick at
the can, so to speak.

00:12:46.981 --> 00:12:49.790
Right. So, these perceptions come in,

00:12:49.791 --> 00:12:52.790
they go straight into
our affective reasoning engine.

00:12:52.791 --> 00:12:55.565
This generates an immediate action.

00:12:55.566 --> 00:12:57.905
This is what I should do next.

00:12:57.906 --> 00:13:01.565
The key behind this
affective reasoning engine,

00:13:01.566 --> 00:13:03.800
is that it provides an action,

00:13:03.801 --> 00:13:06.650
a course of action to take
which is the one that is

00:13:06.651 --> 00:13:10.440
most socially reasonable
in the situation.

00:13:10.441 --> 00:13:14.740
After that, if this breaks down,

00:13:14.741 --> 00:13:18.730
then people can call in these
higher level cognitive processes

00:13:18.731 --> 00:13:22.660
in order to figure
out how to fix this.

00:13:22.661 --> 00:13:24.740
Right. So, okay.

00:13:24.741 --> 00:13:27.605
So, that's the sort of
very high level view.

00:13:27.606 --> 00:13:29.435
This is an emotional machine which is

00:13:29.436 --> 00:13:32.940
disrupted by cognition. Yeah.

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:35.780
&gt;&gt; It is a part what is affective

00:13:35.781 --> 00:13:37.925
here and what is just
sort of a quick,

00:13:37.926 --> 00:13:41.270
a muscle memory like a confidence
computation already before.

00:13:41.271 --> 00:13:42.395
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:13:42.396 --> 00:13:44.300
&gt;&gt; We're separating those two.

00:13:44.301 --> 00:13:45.965
&gt;&gt; Yeah. We're separating those two.

00:13:45.966 --> 00:13:49.700
So, I guess there's actually
a third system here which I'm

00:13:49.701 --> 00:13:53.030
not putting in which
bypasses all of this.

00:13:53.031 --> 00:13:56.780
Right. So, you could draw another
arrow that is this immediate.

00:13:56.781 --> 00:14:00.035
So, that's when if I jump
at you and you jump back,

00:14:00.036 --> 00:14:02.690
that's your instinctive muscle memory

00:14:02.691 --> 00:14:05.720
to avoid things coming
at you, for example.

00:14:05.721 --> 00:14:07.655
That's not what this
is talking about here.

00:14:07.656 --> 00:14:09.965
This is still a cognitive process.

00:14:09.966 --> 00:14:12.710
The theory that I'll
describe to you is,

00:14:12.711 --> 00:14:18.770
this cognitive process
relies heavily on language.

00:14:18.771 --> 00:14:20.780
So, on how we interpret the world in

00:14:20.781 --> 00:14:24.035
terms of symbols, essentially.

00:14:24.036 --> 00:14:27.600
Right. Or linguistic concepts.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.610
Any other thoughts or questions?

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:40.085
So, I'm going to now
describe to you this theory,

00:14:40.086 --> 00:14:42.440
and then I'll give you
a few examples of how I've

00:14:42.441 --> 00:14:45.860
I've gotten it working in
a couple of different contexts.

00:14:45.861 --> 00:14:47.885
So, how are we going to do this,

00:14:47.886 --> 00:14:52.565
is I'm going to look back at
this work by Osgood in the '50s.

00:14:52.566 --> 00:14:54.770
So, Charles Osgood who
was a psychologist

00:14:54.771 --> 00:14:57.530
who did these amazing experiments

00:14:57.531 --> 00:15:02.390
and he went around the world and
asked people to rate concepts.

00:15:02.391 --> 00:15:04.520
So, basically words in the language,

00:15:04.521 --> 00:15:06.230
but he did this in
many different cultures.

00:15:06.231 --> 00:15:07.940
So, different languages.

00:15:07.941 --> 00:15:10.610
He got people to rate them on

00:15:10.611 --> 00:15:13.265
what are called semantic
differential scales.

00:15:13.266 --> 00:15:15.710
Semantic differential scales
are just a scale

00:15:15.711 --> 00:15:18.200
with two opposing words at each end.

00:15:18.201 --> 00:15:20.870
Right. So, angular to rounded,

00:15:20.871 --> 00:15:21.980
or weak to strong,

00:15:21.981 --> 00:15:23.255
or rough to smooth.

00:15:23.256 --> 00:15:25.490
Right. He asked people,

00:15:25.491 --> 00:15:28.295
he'd give them a word like in
this case the word polite,

00:15:28.296 --> 00:15:30.290
and say, put a check mark on each of

00:15:30.291 --> 00:15:34.250
these scales where you
think politeness sets.

00:15:34.251 --> 00:15:38.300
What's amazing is that there's
this incredible degree of

00:15:38.301 --> 00:15:42.140
consistency amongst humans
that share the same language,

00:15:42.141 --> 00:15:43.925
that are in the same cultural group

00:15:43.926 --> 00:15:46.580
as to where these words should go.

00:15:46.581 --> 00:15:51.725
So, this picture show one of
the diagrams from his study in 1952,

00:15:51.726 --> 00:15:53.870
showing two groups of 20 people.

00:15:53.871 --> 00:15:56.105
This is the mean of
two groups of 20 people.

00:15:56.106 --> 00:15:58.355
You can see this amazing
degree of consistency.

00:15:58.356 --> 00:16:00.020
Everybody agrees.

00:16:00.021 --> 00:16:02.975
Right. So, what's amazing
about that is that,

00:16:02.976 --> 00:16:04.700
if you look at the word polite

00:16:04.701 --> 00:16:06.440
and you look at some of these scales,

00:16:06.441 --> 00:16:08.720
so you might wonder,
what is politeness

00:16:08.721 --> 00:16:11.240
has to do with being
rougher, being smooth?

00:16:11.241 --> 00:16:14.750
Right. Like, it seems to
be unrelated in some way,

00:16:14.751 --> 00:16:19.370
at least on a semantic level
but, everybody agrees.

00:16:19.371 --> 00:16:22.625
Yeah. Politeness is more sort
of smooth and rough.

00:16:22.626 --> 00:16:25.100
Right. So, there's something really

00:16:25.101 --> 00:16:27.620
interesting going on
here that everybody has

00:16:27.621 --> 00:16:30.320
this implicit that we all share

00:16:30.321 --> 00:16:36.725
these ratings of things on
these different scales.

00:16:36.726 --> 00:16:38.300
So, what he did was,

00:16:38.301 --> 00:16:39.950
he did these studies
and he actually used

00:16:39.951 --> 00:16:41.870
many more dimensions than this.

00:16:41.871 --> 00:16:43.670
So there's, I think on the order of

00:16:43.671 --> 00:16:48.695
like 150 dimensions that he
used or 150 of these scales.

00:16:48.696 --> 00:16:50.120
Then he went back and did

00:16:50.121 --> 00:16:53.090
a dimensionality reduction and
found that he could account for

00:16:53.091 --> 00:16:54.830
over 80 percent of the variance and

00:16:54.831 --> 00:16:58.265
all this data with
just three dimensions.

00:16:58.266 --> 00:17:00.830
The three dimensions
are one dimension of

00:17:00.831 --> 00:17:03.335
evaluations of good to bad,

00:17:03.336 --> 00:17:05.060
one dimension of power,

00:17:05.061 --> 00:17:06.635
so strong to weak,

00:17:06.636 --> 00:17:08.720
and one dimension of activity,

00:17:08.721 --> 00:17:11.015
so hyper to asleep.

00:17:11.016 --> 00:17:13.340
I'm going to put this one
coming out of the board.

00:17:13.341 --> 00:17:16.550
So, bigger things are more hyper.

00:17:16.551 --> 00:17:19.415
So, when you get
people to rate words,

00:17:19.416 --> 00:17:21.020
they all agree on where they should

00:17:21.021 --> 00:17:22.925
go in this three-dimensional space.

00:17:22.926 --> 00:17:26.150
So, for example, being
polite is good and powerful

00:17:26.151 --> 00:17:30.065
and being abusive is weak
and bad, and really active.

00:17:30.066 --> 00:17:32.720
Right. So, this is a better word.

00:17:32.721 --> 00:17:38.220
Being serene is good and
weak and really inactive.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:41.420
You could do the same thing
with identities.

00:17:41.421 --> 00:17:43.850
So, students and professors

00:17:43.851 --> 00:17:45.860
for example is a good,
that's a good example.

00:17:45.861 --> 00:17:51.365
So, students are, everybody
agrees that students are,

00:17:51.366 --> 00:17:54.485
students and professors are
about the same goodness.

00:17:54.486 --> 00:17:56.510
Right. But maybe
professors are a little

00:17:56.511 --> 00:17:58.670
bit more powerful than students,

00:17:58.671 --> 00:18:01.625
and students are maybe a bit
more active than professors.

00:18:01.626 --> 00:18:04.985
Right. This is culturally dependent.

00:18:04.986 --> 00:18:08.135
Right. So, if you go
to Japan for instance,

00:18:08.136 --> 00:18:10.610
then professors are typically seen

00:18:10.611 --> 00:18:13.990
as significantly more
powerful than students.

00:18:13.991 --> 00:18:16.590
Right. So, but if you
come to North America,

00:18:16.591 --> 00:18:18.410
then this has sort of
become normalized.

00:18:18.411 --> 00:18:19.910
Students and professors
are seen as much

00:18:19.911 --> 00:18:23.045
more equal on this power dimension,

00:18:23.046 --> 00:18:24.950
and behaviors as well.

00:18:24.951 --> 00:18:28.400
So, apologizing to somebody and
fighting with somebody, and so on.

00:18:28.401 --> 00:18:30.860
So, this is actually real data
taken from a study done

00:18:30.861 --> 00:18:33.450
in 2003 in the United States.

00:18:33.451 --> 00:18:35.555
So, these are the means of,

00:18:35.556 --> 00:18:37.100
I put the words that the means of

00:18:37.101 --> 00:18:39.260
where people rate these concepts.

00:18:39.261 --> 00:18:41.120
Again, I'll just reiterate that

00:18:41.121 --> 00:18:42.935
the important thing to remember here,

00:18:42.936 --> 00:18:45.140
is that everybody in

00:18:45.141 --> 00:18:47.180
the cultural group agrees on

00:18:47.181 --> 00:18:50.550
where these words should go in
this three-dimensional space.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:59.590
Okay. So, how are we going to
take this idea, and leverage it,

00:18:59.591 --> 00:19:02.020
and turn it into a
control principle that

00:19:02.021 --> 00:19:05.125
we can use to drive
an artificial agent.

00:19:05.126 --> 00:19:08.650
We're going to do that using
this Affect Control Theory,

00:19:08.651 --> 00:19:11.650
and what Affect Control Theory
says is that people

00:19:11.651 --> 00:19:14.695
carry around these
fundamental sentiments.

00:19:14.696 --> 00:19:17.365
The fundamental sentiments
are these things here.

00:19:17.366 --> 00:19:20.140
This is everybody's agreement
on where things should go

00:19:20.141 --> 00:19:24.080
in this very simple just
three-dimensional space.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.970
When a social action occurs,

00:19:27.971 --> 00:19:30.610
so something happens in the world.

00:19:30.611 --> 00:19:33.250
So, for example, maybe in
this case here we have a nurse,

00:19:33.251 --> 00:19:36.460
and here's a nurse dumping
some water on this guy's head

00:19:36.461 --> 00:19:40.375
and here's a nurse helping
this lady, for example.

00:19:40.376 --> 00:19:44.290
These social actions create what
are called transient impressions.

00:19:44.291 --> 00:19:48.775
So, the transient impressions are
also culturally shared things.

00:19:48.776 --> 00:19:52.060
They are how you feel
about the situation.

00:19:52.061 --> 00:19:56.275
So, how you feel about these things
in context, all right.

00:19:56.276 --> 00:19:58.270
The transient impressions may be

00:19:58.271 --> 00:20:00.370
different from these
fundamental sentiments and

00:20:00.371 --> 00:20:04.585
the difference is called
the deflection, in this theory.

00:20:04.586 --> 00:20:08.110
The Affect Control Principle
says that people try to

00:20:08.111 --> 00:20:11.620
experience events that confirm
fundamental sentiments.

00:20:11.621 --> 00:20:15.010
So, essentially they try
to reduce this deflection.

00:20:15.011 --> 00:20:18.250
Affect Control Theory is
a consistency theory.

00:20:18.251 --> 00:20:19.960
It says that we will try,

00:20:19.961 --> 00:20:22.165
people will basically do the thing

00:20:22.166 --> 00:20:26.065
that leads them to
situations where things are

00:20:26.066 --> 00:20:30.070
emotionally consistent and
the consistency is defined

00:20:30.071 --> 00:20:34.465
by a culturally shared construct,

00:20:34.466 --> 00:20:36.920
which I'll show you in a second.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:45.760
I'm going to go through a couple
of levels of increasing detail of

00:20:45.761 --> 00:20:50.905
this theory and this is at the
high the highest possible level.

00:20:50.906 --> 00:20:54.010
But interrupt me anytime if
you have questions as we go.

00:20:54.011 --> 00:20:57.220
But I'll show you a bit more detail
of this as we go along.

00:20:57.221 --> 00:21:00.130
It's all described in
this really nice book by David Hayes

00:21:00.131 --> 00:21:04.450
who I guess is the guy who's
done many many years of

00:21:04.451 --> 00:21:06.160
work on this theory starting

00:21:06.161 --> 00:21:08.560
from the work of all is good
and many others as well

00:21:08.561 --> 00:21:11.200
and describing this really
nice little book called

00:21:11.201 --> 00:21:12.295
Expressive Order.

00:21:12.296 --> 00:21:15.040
One of the interesting things
about this book is that it comes,

00:21:15.041 --> 00:21:16.465
it has two parts.

00:21:16.466 --> 00:21:19.345
The first part is
all the social psychological part

00:21:19.346 --> 00:21:21.820
and it's tasked and it

00:21:21.821 --> 00:21:24.760
describes his working in
looking at a variety of

00:21:24.761 --> 00:21:28.210
different cultural groups and
how they follow these dynamics,

00:21:28.211 --> 00:21:31.885
and in the second part
is a mathematical model.

00:21:31.886 --> 00:21:35.560
What's interesting is when you
talk to most sociologists,

00:21:35.561 --> 00:21:37.690
they've all read the first
part of the book

00:21:37.691 --> 00:21:40.000
but ignored the second part.

00:21:40.001 --> 00:21:45.655
So, they don't tend to
have that math background.

00:21:45.656 --> 00:21:47.770
When I read it, I read
the first part of

00:21:47.771 --> 00:21:49.570
the book and I was completely lost.

00:21:49.571 --> 00:21:50.530
I didn't know where it was going on.

00:21:50.531 --> 00:21:51.490
But then I read the second part,

00:21:51.491 --> 00:21:53.470
I was like oh, this
makes total sense.

00:21:53.471 --> 00:21:55.750
So, this is why it's good to have

00:21:55.751 --> 00:21:59.575
a multi-disciplinary group
working together.

00:21:59.576 --> 00:22:01.705
Here's a little bit more detail.

00:22:01.706 --> 00:22:03.400
Affect Control Theory uses

00:22:03.401 --> 00:22:06.970
a very simple grammar of an
actor behaving on an object.

00:22:06.971 --> 00:22:11.000
The object is often another person.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:14.410
People have these
fundamental sentiments

00:22:14.411 --> 00:22:16.825
about each of these three things.

00:22:16.826 --> 00:22:19.300
This is a nine dimensional vector

00:22:19.301 --> 00:22:21.490
and it's according to the theory,

00:22:21.491 --> 00:22:27.100
these vectors lie in the range
minus 4.3 plus 4.3 and I've asked

00:22:27.101 --> 00:22:29.770
every sociologist I've met
who works in this theory of

00:22:29.771 --> 00:22:32.815
why it's 4.3 and
nobody seems to know.

00:22:32.816 --> 00:22:35.350
I think it's lost in
the sands of time but it

00:22:35.351 --> 00:22:38.125
does have something to do with all
is good's original experiments.

00:22:38.126 --> 00:22:40.780
It's actually not important
and the versions that I work

00:22:40.781 --> 00:22:43.330
with this not restricted
to be in this range,

00:22:43.331 --> 00:22:46.100
and you can re-scale it
to any that you want it.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:49.630
When you see an actor
behaving on an object,

00:22:49.631 --> 00:22:53.170
it creates a transient impression
for each of them as well,

00:22:53.171 --> 00:22:55.090
and this is again gives you

00:22:55.091 --> 00:22:58.360
a nine dimensional vector
in the same space.

00:22:58.361 --> 00:23:02.440
The predictions of the transients
from the fundamentals are

00:23:02.441 --> 00:23:06.325
given by this non-linear operator
which I'll show you in a second,

00:23:06.326 --> 00:23:10.510
and this is something that
is also culturally shared.

00:23:10.511 --> 00:23:13.825
Everybody agrees on how
these impressions are created.

00:23:13.826 --> 00:23:15.190
The deflection is then

00:23:15.191 --> 00:23:18.549
just the Euclidean distance between
fundamentals and transient,

00:23:18.550 --> 00:23:21.205
and The Affect Control
Principle says that

00:23:21.206 --> 00:23:25.550
actors work to reduce
this deflection.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:30.000
You can use this as a control
principle because you can say part

00:23:30.001 --> 00:23:33.495
of this sentiment is a behavior.

00:23:33.496 --> 00:23:38.625
So, you can say what is the best
behavior to take in order to make

00:23:38.626 --> 00:23:40.649
this actor and this object

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:43.200
agree emotionally to be

00:23:43.201 --> 00:23:45.390
doing something emotionally
that's consistent.

00:23:45.391 --> 00:23:49.470
So, you can use this deflection
measure as an optimization service

00:23:49.471 --> 00:23:54.400
that you can walk down to find
the optimal behavior to take.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.910
Affect Control Theory also has

00:23:56.911 --> 00:24:00.120
a very precise definition of
emotion which is remarkable.

00:24:00.121 --> 00:24:02.100
This is, I think,
the only theory I've ever seen

00:24:02.101 --> 00:24:04.265
that has such a precise definition.

00:24:04.266 --> 00:24:07.360
The emotions are described
as being proportional to

00:24:07.361 --> 00:24:11.530
the vector difference between
fundamentals and transients.

00:24:11.531 --> 00:24:16.900
People use emotions as
a communicative signal to

00:24:16.901 --> 00:24:18.880
tell the person that
they're interacting

00:24:18.881 --> 00:24:22.360
with where that person
is getting it wrong.

00:24:22.361 --> 00:24:25.585
So, if I'm interacting
with you and you're not

00:24:25.586 --> 00:24:28.765
treating me how I think I should
be treated, in this situation,

00:24:28.766 --> 00:24:30.850
I'll send you an emotional signal in

00:24:30.851 --> 00:24:33.100
order to help you correct for that,

00:24:33.101 --> 00:24:35.080
and so that we can
come to an agreement

00:24:35.081 --> 00:24:37.870
on how we're supposed
to be behaving. Yeah.

00:24:37.871 --> 00:24:39.370
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] I guess a vector.

00:24:39.371 --> 00:24:42.580
&gt;&gt; It's a vector in the same
three-dimensional space. Yeah.

00:24:42.581 --> 00:24:45.760
&gt;&gt; So, yeah, I guess
different emotions like happiness,

00:24:45.761 --> 00:24:48.985
sadness [inaudible] are
these points in space?

00:24:48.986 --> 00:24:52.240
&gt;&gt; That's right. They would
be a point in the space.

00:24:52.241 --> 00:24:53.980
If you look back at here,

00:24:53.981 --> 00:24:57.925
that's why these emotion words
are in the same space.

00:24:57.926 --> 00:25:00.910
The nice thing about
this three-dimensional space

00:25:00.911 --> 00:25:02.200
is that you can compare

00:25:02.201 --> 00:25:03.940
all these different entities in

00:25:03.941 --> 00:25:06.790
the same space of behaviors,
identities, emotions.

00:25:06.791 --> 00:25:09.400
They are all comparable directly,

00:25:09.401 --> 00:25:13.070
with a simple
three-dimensional vector.

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:20.845
Here's the little bit more detail.

00:25:20.846 --> 00:25:23.530
So, we have a social
interaction happening here.

00:25:23.531 --> 00:25:25.435
Here's our nurse and our patient.

00:25:25.436 --> 00:25:28.150
We've already done
a population survey.

00:25:28.151 --> 00:25:29.755
So, we've asked people,

00:25:29.756 --> 00:25:32.065
what do you feel about a nurse,

00:25:32.066 --> 00:25:36.010
and we've gathered a rating for
nurses which is this one here.

00:25:36.011 --> 00:25:38.350
So, nurses are seen as 2.9,

00:25:38.351 --> 00:25:40.840
1.5, 0.2, for example.

00:25:40.841 --> 00:25:43.120
Then we've asked them
about patients and

00:25:43.121 --> 00:25:45.655
patients are a little bit less good,

00:25:45.656 --> 00:25:48.340
much less powerful,
and much less active.

00:25:48.341 --> 00:25:51.220
How about comforting,
the behavior of comforting?

00:25:51.221 --> 00:25:54.880
Well, that's positive and
powerful and not very active.

00:25:54.881 --> 00:25:57.310
Given these fundamental sentiments,

00:25:57.311 --> 00:26:00.550
we compute a transient impression

00:26:00.551 --> 00:26:03.670
for each of the three and
the transient impression is given by

00:26:03.671 --> 00:26:10.180
a series of equations with
polynomial features in them.

00:26:10.181 --> 00:26:11.770
So, the polynomials are basically,

00:26:11.771 --> 00:26:14.230
these polynomial features
are combinations of

00:26:14.231 --> 00:26:17.755
these different elements in
the fundamental sentiment.

00:26:17.756 --> 00:26:20.320
So, this one here is
the evaluation of

00:26:20.321 --> 00:26:23.860
the behavior multiplied by
the evaluation of the object,

00:26:23.861 --> 00:26:26.770
and then it has a positive
coefficient and that's

00:26:26.771 --> 00:26:30.205
the effect on the
evaluation of the actor.

00:26:30.206 --> 00:26:33.430
What this term says is that if

00:26:33.431 --> 00:26:37.734
the actor does something
good to a good person,

00:26:37.735 --> 00:26:40.300
then they will seem more good.

00:26:40.301 --> 00:26:43.030
These coefficients
are things that are

00:26:43.031 --> 00:26:45.130
also culturally shared
and measurable.

00:26:45.131 --> 00:26:46.750
So, we can ask people,

00:26:46.751 --> 00:26:49.030
how do you feel about a nurse,

00:26:49.031 --> 00:26:50.500
how do you feel about a patient,

00:26:50.501 --> 00:26:52.930
how do you feel about
the act of comforting,

00:26:52.931 --> 00:26:54.880
how do you feel about
a nurse when you

00:26:54.881 --> 00:26:57.595
see a nurse comforting a patient ,

00:26:57.596 --> 00:27:01.045
and that measures
this transient impression

00:27:01.046 --> 00:27:04.135
and then they can do a regression
and find these coefficients,

00:27:04.136 --> 00:27:08.600
and find that they are
again shared culturally.

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:12.895
So, the difference between
these two things is the deflection.

00:27:12.896 --> 00:27:16.390
Like I said before, I can use that
to predict the next behavior.

00:27:16.391 --> 00:27:17.680
So I can optimize over

00:27:17.681 --> 00:27:19.675
this deflection measure
in order to have

00:27:19.676 --> 00:27:21.970
compute the best behavior to take.

00:27:21.971 --> 00:27:23.320
I can also compute

00:27:23.321 --> 00:27:26.785
a re-identification of
the nurse and the patient.

00:27:26.786 --> 00:27:28.720
So, I can say, if I see

00:27:28.721 --> 00:27:31.645
somebody comforting
a patient, who might that be?

00:27:31.646 --> 00:27:34.045
Well, that's probably a nurse.

00:27:34.046 --> 00:27:36.800
So, I can do that as well.

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:41.980
Before we go to an example
which might help

00:27:41.981 --> 00:27:45.650
clarify some of this, any questions?

00:27:47.870 --> 00:27:50.430
All right. So, let's
walk through an example.

00:27:50.431 --> 00:27:52.620
I will use this
nurse-patient example.

00:27:52.621 --> 00:27:55.005
Here's our nurse and our patient.

00:27:55.006 --> 00:27:59.159
Again, here's their 3-D
evaluation potency

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:02.055
and activity fundamental sentiments.

00:28:02.056 --> 00:28:05.190
Remember, these are
culturally shared.

00:28:05.191 --> 00:28:06.540
I'll just keep reiterating that,

00:28:06.541 --> 00:28:08.445
so we're sure we remember it.

00:28:08.446 --> 00:28:10.350
The nurse is up here,

00:28:10.351 --> 00:28:12.645
and the patient is here.

00:28:12.646 --> 00:28:16.800
The important thing is that
the nurse knows that she is here

00:28:16.801 --> 00:28:19.005
and knows that the patient
is here and the patient

00:28:19.006 --> 00:28:21.780
knows that she's here and
knows that the nurse is here.

00:28:21.781 --> 00:28:24.610
They both agree on that.

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:28.050
So, I can compute.
Well, let's see what

00:28:28.051 --> 00:28:30.435
happens if the nurse
comforts the patient.

00:28:30.436 --> 00:28:33.915
So, I compute the transient
impressions which are in light blue,

00:28:33.916 --> 00:28:37.155
and you can see they're quite close
to the fundamental sentiments,

00:28:37.156 --> 00:28:39.420
and then I can compute
what emotions would

00:28:39.421 --> 00:28:42.000
be felt and communicated.

00:28:42.001 --> 00:28:44.040
The nurse who comforts
the patient would feel

00:28:44.041 --> 00:28:47.580
compassionate and the patient
would feel serene.

00:28:47.581 --> 00:28:49.440
The reflection is low,

00:28:49.441 --> 00:28:52.510
it's a 1.3 so that's
a low deflection.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:56.490
But let's say we make
the nurse ignore the patient.

00:28:56.491 --> 00:28:58.650
Here's a nurse who's
ignoring a patient.

00:28:58.651 --> 00:29:03.510
Ignoring is quite negative
and not very powerful.

00:29:03.511 --> 00:29:09.270
Now, the nurse seems much more bad
than a normal nurse would seem.

00:29:09.271 --> 00:29:10.965
We'd say, "Well, who is this?

00:29:10.966 --> 00:29:12.540
This is not a very nice nurse.

00:29:12.541 --> 00:29:14.655
She's ignoring this poor patient."

00:29:14.656 --> 00:29:17.850
But the patient also
seems more bad because,

00:29:17.851 --> 00:29:21.120
well, maybe this patient has
been a really nasty patient.

00:29:21.121 --> 00:29:23.220
That's why the nurse is ignoring her.

00:29:23.221 --> 00:29:25.500
So, we construct all of these things,

00:29:25.501 --> 00:29:29.385
but we do so just by computing
these transient impressions.

00:29:29.386 --> 00:29:31.365
It's a very simple calculation.

00:29:31.366 --> 00:29:33.570
The emotions that will be created or

00:29:33.571 --> 00:29:36.105
agitation for the nurse
and the indignant,

00:29:36.106 --> 00:29:40.600
patient feels indignant, "How dare
you ignore me, I'm the patient."

00:29:42.110 --> 00:29:45.930
Remember, these are
the signals that each

00:29:45.931 --> 00:29:49.155
would send to the other in
order to correct for this.

00:29:49.156 --> 00:29:54.330
So, the patient would send
this vector to the nurse and

00:29:54.331 --> 00:29:56.580
say to the nurse
essentially by sending

00:29:56.581 --> 00:30:00.390
that emotional signal which
humans can do very quickly,

00:30:00.391 --> 00:30:02.700
"Hey, listen, I'm
supposed to be up here

00:30:02.701 --> 00:30:05.170
and you're treating me
like I'm down here,

00:30:05.171 --> 00:30:07.550
and I'm telling you
exactly how you're

00:30:07.551 --> 00:30:10.070
treating me by sending
you this vector."

00:30:10.071 --> 00:30:12.275
The nurse can take that and say,

00:30:12.276 --> 00:30:13.880
"Right, I got it wrong.

00:30:13.881 --> 00:30:17.680
I thought you were horrible patient
but really you're not."

00:30:17.681 --> 00:30:20.490
So, I'll correct for that
and do something to make it

00:30:20.491 --> 00:30:26.350
better. Deflection is high.

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:33.135
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] so if
you ignore again.

00:30:33.136 --> 00:30:36.255
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so what it means on

00:30:36.256 --> 00:30:41.700
the behaviors is that you
may interpret the behavior.

00:30:41.701 --> 00:30:43.590
If you're seeing what's happening,

00:30:43.591 --> 00:30:45.615
then you're at least made

00:30:45.616 --> 00:30:47.550
I'm sure that's a nurse, and
I'm sure that's a patient.

00:30:47.551 --> 00:30:49.635
Maybe she's not ignoring the patient.

00:30:49.636 --> 00:30:53.790
You seek an interpretation

00:30:53.791 --> 00:30:57.210
of the situation that makes
it emotionally consistent.

00:30:57.211 --> 00:30:59.580
So, the transient impression
would be one way

00:30:59.581 --> 00:31:03.370
of getting that from the behavior.

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:11.625
So, for example, well, you see here,

00:31:11.626 --> 00:31:14.745
this has gone up in evaluation,

00:31:14.746 --> 00:31:17.130
and it's gone up in power.

00:31:17.131 --> 00:31:19.830
So, one possible, well,
maybe this is not,

00:31:19.831 --> 00:31:21.690
I don't know what word
would be close to

00:31:21.691 --> 00:31:23.940
this transient impression but it's

00:31:23.941 --> 00:31:28.260
probably something last like
ignoring, something more positive.

00:31:28.261 --> 00:31:32.040
Well, maybe she's busy and just
come back to this patient later or

00:31:32.041 --> 00:31:36.730
something like that. That make sense.

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:43.170
All right. Here's, another example.

00:31:43.171 --> 00:31:45.750
This is a student and
a automated tutor.

00:31:45.751 --> 00:31:48.990
This is one of the early examples
that we built with this theory.

00:31:48.991 --> 00:31:51.705
The student and the tutor are,

00:31:51.706 --> 00:31:53.565
this is student and professor,

00:31:53.566 --> 00:31:56.130
they're in the same spot
but maybe the tutor

00:31:56.131 --> 00:31:59.145
has a bit of power over the student.

00:31:59.146 --> 00:32:03.450
So, we can compute. Well, what
would a student do to a professor.

00:32:03.451 --> 00:32:07.845
Well, the student would do something
like this EP and A rating,

00:32:07.846 --> 00:32:08.970
and I can look up in

00:32:08.971 --> 00:32:12.390
my dictionary of sentiments
that I've had rated by

00:32:12.391 --> 00:32:14.550
people and find this behavior or

00:32:14.551 --> 00:32:17.940
the student might ask
something to the tutor.

00:32:17.941 --> 00:32:20.040
Then, the deflection is low.

00:32:20.041 --> 00:32:21.300
The transients are close to

00:32:21.301 --> 00:32:23.550
the fundamentals, and
everything's good.

00:32:23.551 --> 00:32:27.705
What if the tutor
yells at the student?

00:32:27.706 --> 00:32:30.180
This creates transient impressions

00:32:30.181 --> 00:32:32.580
that are very far from
these fundamentals.

00:32:32.581 --> 00:32:37.290
Maybe this student is really a lot
more or less is a bad student,

00:32:37.291 --> 00:32:40.540
or maybe this is a horrible tutor.

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:44.250
The obvious, what emotion would

00:32:44.251 --> 00:32:46.515
be felt or maybe this
tutor is angry and

00:32:46.516 --> 00:32:48.495
this student would be exasperated or

00:32:48.496 --> 00:32:51.560
remorseful. Then we can compute.

00:32:51.561 --> 00:32:53.210
Well, what would
the student do in order to

00:32:53.211 --> 00:32:55.190
try to correct for this situation.

00:32:55.191 --> 00:32:57.050
Well, they would maybe listen to

00:32:57.051 --> 00:33:00.845
the tutor and feel self-conscious,

00:33:00.846 --> 00:33:04.945
and this acts by the student
would help it to restore

00:33:04.946 --> 00:33:07.710
his transient
impressions back towards

00:33:07.711 --> 00:33:09.540
its fundamental sentiments but not so

00:33:09.541 --> 00:33:11.595
much for the tutor because
the jury is still up.

00:33:11.596 --> 00:33:13.350
The student doesn't know maybe

00:33:13.351 --> 00:33:16.275
this tutor is really
a nasty tutor after all.

00:33:16.276 --> 00:33:18.480
But if the tutor then does something,

00:33:18.481 --> 00:33:20.160
so this is the computation,

00:33:20.161 --> 00:33:21.840
the optimization for the deflection.

00:33:21.841 --> 00:33:23.955
So, it's minimizing this deflection.

00:33:23.956 --> 00:33:25.500
Well, what would the tutor do?

00:33:25.501 --> 00:33:28.665
Something like apologize to
the student and now they

00:33:28.666 --> 00:33:30.150
manage to get back to

00:33:30.151 --> 00:33:33.550
somewhere where the
deflection is fairly low.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:37.320
So what I did here
in this example was,

00:33:37.321 --> 00:33:39.420
I forced the tutor to
yell at the student.

00:33:39.421 --> 00:33:41.880
That's not an action that
Affect Control Theory

00:33:41.881 --> 00:33:45.255
would compute because it
creates so much deflection.

00:33:45.256 --> 00:33:49.860
I create that artificial
action and then

00:33:49.861 --> 00:33:51.690
compute what's the optimal thing for

00:33:51.691 --> 00:33:54.330
the student to do to
try to fix things,

00:33:54.331 --> 00:33:55.860
and then what's the optimal thing for

00:33:55.861 --> 00:33:59.110
the tutor to do to try
to fix things even more.

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:06.820
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:34:06.821 --> 00:34:08.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there could be

00:34:08.101 --> 00:34:09.690
circumstances where
it's appropriate, yeah,

00:34:09.691 --> 00:34:11.730
and people could yell at each

00:34:11.731 --> 00:34:14.220
other for a variety
of different reasons.

00:34:14.221 --> 00:34:16.665
One of them is, maybe not yelling at,

00:34:16.666 --> 00:34:19.245
maybe that's a little bit too strong.

00:34:19.246 --> 00:34:23.190
Yeah. One of the things that
happens when deflection

00:34:23.191 --> 00:34:27.495
goes up is that this system
to thinking kicks in.

00:34:27.496 --> 00:34:31.230
So, when there is a inconsistency
on the emotional.

00:34:31.231 --> 00:34:32.970
So, the basic idea is that,

00:34:32.971 --> 00:34:36.855
if I'm operating, this is
like an equilibrium theory.

00:34:36.856 --> 00:34:39.510
So, if we're on
the same page and we're

00:34:39.511 --> 00:34:42.030
operating according to
this emotional model,

00:34:42.031 --> 00:34:45.645
as long as everything stays
at that equilibrium point,

00:34:45.646 --> 00:34:47.055
then we'll just keep doing that.

00:34:47.056 --> 00:34:48.630
It's really easy. You
don't have to think.

00:34:48.631 --> 00:34:51.525
You just have to operate in
this simple three-dimensional space.

00:34:51.526 --> 00:34:54.360
As soon as things go
off the rails though,

00:34:54.361 --> 00:34:57.945
then you kick in this higher
level cognitive reasoning.

00:34:57.946 --> 00:35:01.500
One of the things
that that might help

00:35:01.501 --> 00:35:02.910
with is that it might force

00:35:02.911 --> 00:35:05.160
the student to actually think
about what they're learning,

00:35:05.161 --> 00:35:09.090
for example, and then they would
actually consider it and take

00:35:09.091 --> 00:35:11.310
the information in instead of just

00:35:11.311 --> 00:35:14.295
ignoring it and pretending
like everything's fine.

00:35:14.296 --> 00:35:17.370
So, that could be one reason. Yeah.

00:35:17.371 --> 00:35:19.560
&gt;&gt; So, just, I guess maybe to

00:35:19.561 --> 00:35:22.810
continue on the light
of what you just said,

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:26.670
is there an implication
may be that in

00:35:26.671 --> 00:35:30.870
some scenarios there's
utility in having

00:35:30.871 --> 00:35:34.500
high deflection because
it forces you to engage

00:35:34.501 --> 00:35:36.270
that higher level system and start

00:35:36.271 --> 00:35:39.250
thinking more deeply about something?

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:41.625
&gt;&gt; No, there could be for sure.

00:35:41.626 --> 00:35:45.150
Yeah. I'll talk about that
a bit more in a second.

00:35:45.151 --> 00:35:50.130
But Affect Control Theory
doesn't have a utility function.

00:35:50.131 --> 00:35:52.515
The version that I've developed,

00:35:52.516 --> 00:35:53.700
which I'll show you next,

00:35:53.701 --> 00:35:56.250
does but Affect Control Theory

00:35:56.251 --> 00:35:59.385
is only describing what's happening
at that equilibrium point.

00:35:59.386 --> 00:36:02.070
So, let's talk about utility
again after I show you this.

00:36:02.071 --> 00:36:08.410
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] .

00:36:08.411 --> 00:36:09.190
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:36:09.191 --> 00:36:11.260
&gt;&gt; So the teacher
needs to [inaudible].

00:36:11.261 --> 00:36:11.800
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:36:11.801 --> 00:36:12.310
&gt;&gt; Have something [inaudible]

00:36:12.311 --> 00:36:16.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:36:16.781 --> 00:36:18.640
A student staring at the window

00:36:18.641 --> 00:36:20.380
is not being a very
good student, right?

00:36:20.381 --> 00:36:26.575
So, they're already off the
equilibrium point, all right?

00:36:26.576 --> 00:36:28.390
&gt;&gt; I'm just trying to think of
it. Is there a circumstance

00:36:28.391 --> 00:36:30.310
where an action is negatively

00:36:30.311 --> 00:36:35.110
one context, but positive enough?

00:36:35.111 --> 00:36:39.980
&gt;&gt; Yes, I try to think
of a good example there.

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:48.265
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] any action at the [inaudible]
equilibrium is forbid in any action?

00:36:48.266 --> 00:36:49.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:49.331 --> 00:36:57.115
&gt;&gt; We're just looking at yells at
[inaudible] lower left quadrant.

00:36:57.116 --> 00:37:00.010
So, the student wasn't [inaudible].

00:37:00.011 --> 00:37:04.000
It seemed like they yelled
at bring into equilibrium?

00:37:04.001 --> 00:37:08.200
&gt;&gt; This getting a transient

00:37:08.201 --> 00:37:10.420
that is here from here is
only based on this point.

00:37:10.421 --> 00:37:12.010
So, if the student
was actually up here,

00:37:12.011 --> 00:37:13.300
then yelling out wouldn't necessarily

00:37:13.301 --> 00:37:15.385
bring them in the same spot, right?

00:37:15.386 --> 00:37:18.400
But you're right. It could be
something along those lines.

00:37:18.401 --> 00:37:20.570
Maybe a better example is,

00:37:20.571 --> 00:37:26.560
the work that's done in
cognitive therapies for depression.

00:37:26.561 --> 00:37:30.040
So a depressed person
is someone who has

00:37:30.041 --> 00:37:33.490
a very negative sense
of who they are,

00:37:33.491 --> 00:37:36.655
so they feel kind of
negatively about themselves.

00:37:36.656 --> 00:37:39.610
For many years people thought that,

00:37:39.611 --> 00:37:41.620
the way to handle this was to blast

00:37:41.621 --> 00:37:46.660
the person with positive thoughts.

00:37:46.661 --> 00:37:48.295
That doesn't work very well,

00:37:48.296 --> 00:37:50.380
and the reason it doesn't work
very well is because you're not

00:37:50.381 --> 00:37:53.050
treating the depressed person
like a depressed person.

00:37:53.051 --> 00:37:55.730
That's how they think they are.

00:37:56.220 --> 00:38:00.085
So the self-verification theory
came along and said that, "Well,

00:38:00.086 --> 00:38:02.230
what you need to do is
agree with the person

00:38:02.231 --> 00:38:04.450
that the world is terrible
and that everything sucks,

00:38:04.451 --> 00:38:06.100
" and then you say,

00:38:06.101 --> 00:38:07.660
"Yes, this is the way you are."

00:38:07.661 --> 00:38:13.825
I am verifying your sense of who
you are as a depressed person.

00:38:13.826 --> 00:38:16.180
So, I'm performing negative actions,

00:38:16.181 --> 00:38:18.910
but I'm doing it in a
way in order to get us

00:38:18.911 --> 00:38:22.585
basically at that point
of emotional alignment.

00:38:22.586 --> 00:38:24.100
Then, from there, I'm going to

00:38:24.101 --> 00:38:25.990
slowly try to work you in a direction

00:38:25.991 --> 00:38:30.290
where you hopefully become
less depressed, for example.

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:40.030
That makes sense? Okay. All right.

00:38:40.031 --> 00:38:42.580
So, my work in this has been to

00:38:42.581 --> 00:38:47.500
develop a version that adds
a couple of new pieces to this.

00:38:47.501 --> 00:38:49.000
One is a utility function,

00:38:49.001 --> 00:38:50.920
and the other one is modeling

00:38:50.921 --> 00:38:54.010
identities and behaviors as
probability distributions.

00:38:54.011 --> 00:38:57.460
So, of course, it's not the case
that everybody does agree

00:38:57.461 --> 00:38:59.380
exactly on where these concepts

00:38:59.381 --> 00:39:01.030
sit in this three-dimensional space.

00:39:01.031 --> 00:39:02.904
So, when you make these measurements,

00:39:02.905 --> 00:39:05.875
you actually get a fairly big
variance around the mean.

00:39:05.876 --> 00:39:07.975
So everybody does agree on the mean,

00:39:07.976 --> 00:39:11.305
but there's a lot of disagreement
on where they would go, okay?

00:39:11.306 --> 00:39:14.755
There's various turns out
to be very very important.

00:39:14.756 --> 00:39:17.725
So it's something that
people I believe use,

00:39:17.726 --> 00:39:22.720
in order to handle
operating in groups.

00:39:22.721 --> 00:39:25.690
So, the problem with
group behavior is

00:39:25.691 --> 00:39:28.990
that everybody is different,
there's this diversity.

00:39:28.991 --> 00:39:32.440
Even if you're in
a single cultural group.

00:39:32.441 --> 00:39:34.180
So, we as academics are often in

00:39:34.181 --> 00:39:36.265
groups that involve
many different cultures.

00:39:36.266 --> 00:39:39.685
But even if we're in
a very uniform cultural group,

00:39:39.686 --> 00:39:41.440
there's all sorts of
differences because

00:39:41.441 --> 00:39:43.285
people are interacting
with each other in

00:39:43.286 --> 00:39:45.880
many different circles
and it's sort of maybe in

00:39:45.881 --> 00:39:49.000
a small world type social network.

00:39:49.001 --> 00:39:52.855
Because of that, there's lots of
uncertainty that comes into play,

00:39:52.856 --> 00:39:55.690
and we need to be able to model
that uncertainty in order

00:39:55.691 --> 00:40:00.130
to use this kind of idea,

00:40:00.131 --> 00:40:04.520
this emotional consistency to
cooperate with other people.

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.450
Okay. So, those are

00:40:07.451 --> 00:40:10.930
the two pieces and I'll walk you
through how this works as well.

00:40:10.931 --> 00:40:13.330
Quite a few number of papers on it,

00:40:13.331 --> 00:40:15.220
but two of the journal papers,

00:40:15.221 --> 00:40:16.990
one in Artificial
Intelligence, the other one,

00:40:16.991 --> 00:40:19.810
as we've pointed out in
the American Sociological Review,

00:40:19.811 --> 00:40:25.550
and this paper won two awards
at the ESA meetings in 2016.

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:29.190
All right. So, the model is based on

00:40:29.191 --> 00:40:31.200
a partially observable
Markov decision process,

00:40:31.201 --> 00:40:33.660
so I'll give you what
this is all about,

00:40:33.661 --> 00:40:36.960
and then we'll see how we can fit
this affect control theory piece

00:40:36.961 --> 00:40:41.545
onto a generic model of how an
agent interacting with the world.

00:40:41.546 --> 00:40:43.720
So, upon DP or

00:40:43.721 --> 00:40:46.660
partially observable Markov decision
process consists of a set of

00:40:46.661 --> 00:40:49.570
states X and a set

00:40:49.571 --> 00:40:51.820
of observations that we can
make about those states.

00:40:51.821 --> 00:40:54.445
So, these are unobservable
or latent states.

00:40:54.446 --> 00:40:56.125
A set of observations.

00:40:56.126 --> 00:40:57.910
We have an observation function,

00:40:57.911 --> 00:41:01.325
which is a probability distribution
relating these two things.

00:41:01.326 --> 00:41:04.050
There is a set of actions
that an agent can

00:41:04.051 --> 00:41:07.650
take that changed the world according
to some transition function,

00:41:07.651 --> 00:41:10.320
which is another
probability distribution.

00:41:10.321 --> 00:41:12.810
Then, we have some reward or

00:41:12.811 --> 00:41:15.460
utility function that describes

00:41:15.461 --> 00:41:18.205
the agent's preferences
over these states,

00:41:18.206 --> 00:41:20.515
using a numeric scale.

00:41:20.516 --> 00:41:24.065
Okay. So, that's the generic model.

00:41:24.066 --> 00:41:26.895
You can use this to do
a number of different things,

00:41:26.896 --> 00:41:30.450
and maybe the simplest
way of visualizing how

00:41:30.451 --> 00:41:34.215
you can use a POMDP
to generate a plan,

00:41:34.216 --> 00:41:35.520
is to just do a very,

00:41:35.521 --> 00:41:37.325
very simple forward search.

00:41:37.326 --> 00:41:38.830
In general, this doesn't work,

00:41:38.831 --> 00:41:39.970
but this is a way of

00:41:39.971 --> 00:41:42.400
describing what you
actually are trying to do.

00:41:42.401 --> 00:41:44.770
So here we are in our current state,

00:41:44.771 --> 00:41:48.850
and remember this state since this X,

00:41:48.851 --> 00:41:52.795
are sort of interpretation
of the world, is latent.

00:41:52.796 --> 00:41:54.400
We can't observe it directly,

00:41:54.401 --> 00:41:56.950
then the state that we're in
is actually a belief state,

00:41:56.951 --> 00:42:00.430
so this is a probability distribution
over all possible states.

00:42:00.431 --> 00:42:02.770
From this state, my agent

00:42:02.771 --> 00:42:05.290
has a number of different actions
that it can take,

00:42:05.291 --> 00:42:10.645
which lead it to another belief
state after taking the action.

00:42:10.646 --> 00:42:12.910
Then, the other person
that the agent is

00:42:12.911 --> 00:42:15.460
interacting with has a bunch
of things that it can do,

00:42:15.461 --> 00:42:18.235
and that leads to another state,
and so on and so forth.

00:42:18.236 --> 00:42:22.555
Down somewhere in this tree
is some rewards, let's say.

00:42:22.556 --> 00:42:27.100
Then, I can draw this whole tree
out, find these rewards,

00:42:27.101 --> 00:42:29.350
and then propagate them
back up through the tree

00:42:29.351 --> 00:42:32.095
waiting them by the probability
of these things happening.

00:42:32.096 --> 00:42:35.650
Then, I can use that to rank
the actions that my agent has,

00:42:35.651 --> 00:42:40.000
and pick the one that optimizes
the over the utility function.

00:42:40.001 --> 00:42:45.010
So that's just basic Bayesian
decision theory. All right.

00:42:45.011 --> 00:42:46.540
So, what's the problem
with doing this,

00:42:46.541 --> 00:42:47.650
of course, is this tree.

00:42:47.651 --> 00:42:49.630
You can't write it
out, it's way too big.

00:42:49.631 --> 00:42:52.495
These branching factors
are colossal, right?

00:42:52.496 --> 00:42:54.010
So, if you tried to do this,

00:42:54.011 --> 00:42:58.525
you'd rapidly run
into major problems.

00:42:58.526 --> 00:43:02.590
A lot of the work in reinforcement
learning and decision theory

00:43:02.591 --> 00:43:06.370
is how do you expand
this tree in a smart way,

00:43:06.371 --> 00:43:11.380
so that you don't wind up having
to draw the whole thing out.

00:43:11.381 --> 00:43:14.815
So, for example, you
can do things, well,

00:43:14.816 --> 00:43:16.975
as far as what the other
person is going to do,

00:43:16.976 --> 00:43:20.005
I'm just going to ignore all the
things that are really unlikely.

00:43:20.006 --> 00:43:23.440
That could reduce the branching
factor here by doing that.

00:43:23.441 --> 00:43:26.650
But, this branching factor
is much harder to handle

00:43:26.651 --> 00:43:30.430
because I can't ignore any action
that I have available to me.

00:43:30.431 --> 00:43:33.175
The reason is, if I
ignore one action,

00:43:33.176 --> 00:43:35.470
it could be that that is
the action that leads to

00:43:35.471 --> 00:43:37.570
an infinitely higher reward
than anything I've

00:43:37.571 --> 00:43:39.970
seen before, all right?

00:43:39.971 --> 00:43:42.190
So, that's the reinforcement
learning problem,

00:43:42.191 --> 00:43:43.840
I've gotta try them all.

00:43:43.841 --> 00:43:47.080
If I don't, I might miss
the one that is going

00:43:47.081 --> 00:43:52.070
to be utopia for me.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:56.620
All right. So, now
we're going to put in

00:43:56.621 --> 00:43:59.800
this social and emotional
reasoning engine

00:43:59.801 --> 00:44:01.720
and see how we can use that to help

00:44:01.721 --> 00:44:04.720
us guide that action
selection, right?

00:44:04.721 --> 00:44:06.280
Remember back to
the start of the talk,

00:44:06.281 --> 00:44:09.100
we talked about this emotional
reasoning engine is going

00:44:09.101 --> 00:44:12.985
to tell us roughly what to do
in order to get by, right?

00:44:12.986 --> 00:44:14.530
Then we're going to do a bunch of

00:44:14.531 --> 00:44:18.235
maybe cognitive calculations
after that to help us out.

00:44:18.236 --> 00:44:22.060
So, I'm going to take my basic
prime bp and I'm going to just make

00:44:22.061 --> 00:44:23.710
a slight modification
in order to make

00:44:23.711 --> 00:44:25.900
the presentation a little bit easier.

00:44:25.901 --> 00:44:29.800
I'm going to add a random variable
that describes my own action.

00:44:29.801 --> 00:44:31.685
I'll call this b prime,

00:44:31.686 --> 00:44:34.710
and it's just
deterministically set by a.

00:44:34.711 --> 00:44:37.590
So, there is no change
at all in the model,

00:44:37.591 --> 00:44:40.535
I'm just adding this
one random variable.

00:44:40.536 --> 00:44:44.530
Then I'm going to layer in
a set of random variables.

00:44:44.531 --> 00:44:47.320
One for fundamentals
and one for transients,

00:44:47.321 --> 00:44:49.960
and I'm going to connect
them through time.

00:44:49.961 --> 00:44:52.945
So the transients are going
to evolve through time

00:44:52.946 --> 00:44:56.245
according to this impression
formation equation.

00:44:56.246 --> 00:44:58.000
So, I say when do I expect

00:44:58.001 --> 00:45:02.290
the transient impressions to be
after I see an event taking place?

00:45:02.291 --> 00:45:05.770
The fundamental sentiments will
evolve through time as well.

00:45:05.771 --> 00:45:07.330
I will admit the fact that

00:45:07.331 --> 00:45:10.240
these fundamentals may
be changing slightly.

00:45:10.241 --> 00:45:12.040
So if I, for example,

00:45:12.041 --> 00:45:14.275
go and start living in
a different culture,

00:45:14.276 --> 00:45:15.850
then I need to sort of

00:45:15.851 --> 00:45:18.220
shift my fundamental
sentiments a little bit,

00:45:18.221 --> 00:45:20.845
so I'll admit that may be happening.

00:45:20.846 --> 00:45:23.560
Then I have this red link
between the two

00:45:23.561 --> 00:45:25.990
which is my Affect Control Principle.

00:45:25.991 --> 00:45:28.570
It says that I believe

00:45:28.571 --> 00:45:32.545
that fundamentals and transients
will be close together.

00:45:32.546 --> 00:45:35.020
Then I have these green links,

00:45:35.021 --> 00:45:38.905
which are my shared cultural
sentiments about things.

00:45:38.906 --> 00:45:41.230
So, essentially saying that things

00:45:41.231 --> 00:45:43.960
in the world have
fundamental sentiments,

00:45:43.961 --> 00:45:47.110
and that's another probability
distribution and that can

00:45:47.111 --> 00:45:51.010
take into account the variance
in these fundamental sentiments.

00:45:51.011 --> 00:45:53.230
So, given all that,

00:45:53.231 --> 00:45:55.120
I can combine them
together and compute

00:45:55.121 --> 00:45:57.715
a probability distribution
over fundamentals,

00:45:57.716 --> 00:46:02.380
given all of these things
and then I can integrate out

00:46:02.381 --> 00:46:06.235
the identities and create
a distribution just

00:46:06.236 --> 00:46:10.930
over the behaviors in
this three-dimensional space.

00:46:10.931 --> 00:46:16.210
This probability distribution I've
actually labeled as pi because

00:46:16.211 --> 00:46:20.815
it's a policy in a way it
tells me what to do next.

00:46:20.816 --> 00:46:23.410
This is a probability
distribution in E,

00:46:23.411 --> 00:46:24.835
P, and A space,

00:46:24.836 --> 00:46:26.710
but it's telling me this is

00:46:26.711 --> 00:46:31.600
the emotional signature of the thing
to do next in this situation,

00:46:31.601 --> 00:46:33.805
as a probability distribution.

00:46:33.806 --> 00:46:35.350
So then I can draw a sample from

00:46:35.351 --> 00:46:38.665
this and that's the thing
I should do next.

00:46:38.666 --> 00:46:43.670
All right. Or I can seek
the maximum or something like that.

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:52.900
That makes sense? Great. All right.

00:46:52.901 --> 00:46:54.145
So, how does this help us?

00:46:54.146 --> 00:46:56.515
Well, essentially
from a belief state,

00:46:56.516 --> 00:47:00.865
it tells us this function
here says, "Well,

00:47:00.866 --> 00:47:04.435
you don't have to actually
consider every possible action,

00:47:04.436 --> 00:47:06.610
you should consider actions only that

00:47:06.611 --> 00:47:10.870
have this emotional signature."

00:47:10.871 --> 00:47:12.820
Because those are the ones that make

00:47:12.821 --> 00:47:15.565
sense to the people that
you're interacting with.

00:47:15.566 --> 00:47:17.350
They are the socially acceptable

00:47:17.351 --> 00:47:19.765
behaviors that you should be taking.

00:47:19.766 --> 00:47:22.570
So, the way I imagine this is that

00:47:22.571 --> 00:47:25.570
imagine this huge tree is
in the dark which it is,

00:47:25.571 --> 00:47:29.680
because we can't draw
the whole thing out and this affect

00:47:29.681 --> 00:47:31.660
the reasoning engine
is like shining a beam

00:47:31.661 --> 00:47:34.120
of light down through this tree.

00:47:34.121 --> 00:47:38.410
So it's saying, these are the things
you should consider next,

00:47:38.411 --> 00:47:41.515
don't even think about
these things over here.

00:47:41.516 --> 00:47:44.050
So, it's an attentional mechanism.

00:47:44.051 --> 00:47:47.170
It's not a way of ranking
all of the actions,

00:47:47.171 --> 00:47:50.755
it's a way of saying if I draw
a sample from this distribution,

00:47:50.756 --> 00:47:53.410
I'm going to get something
that's in this flashlight beam.

00:47:53.411 --> 00:47:54.850
The things in this flashlight

00:47:54.851 --> 00:47:55.960
beam are the things that are going to

00:47:55.961 --> 00:47:59.830
work at this emotional
consistency level.

00:47:59.831 --> 00:48:03.460
So, essentially, this is a
way of solving this problem

00:48:03.461 --> 00:48:07.480
by having this shared cultural
information on the side,

00:48:07.481 --> 00:48:11.510
that we're using in order
to guide attention.

00:48:13.620 --> 00:48:18.310
Okay. This same idea actually comes,

00:48:18.311 --> 00:48:23.815
you see it in another branch of
sociological practice theory,

00:48:23.816 --> 00:48:27.520
which is fairly far removed,

00:48:27.521 --> 00:48:30.120
it seems from
Affect Control Theory or

00:48:30.121 --> 00:48:33.660
at least the practitioners of
these theories would say so,

00:48:33.661 --> 00:48:36.660
but what's remarkable is that
Bourdieu was mainly one of

00:48:36.661 --> 00:48:39.600
the big originators or thinkers in

00:48:39.601 --> 00:48:42.910
practice theory described
something called

00:48:42.911 --> 00:48:46.870
the Habitus which is very very
similar to what this is doing.

00:48:46.871 --> 00:48:48.475
So I'll just read you this quote,

00:48:48.476 --> 00:48:50.410
"Responses of the Habitus may be

00:48:50.411 --> 00:48:53.320
accompanied by
a strategic calculation,

00:48:53.321 --> 00:48:54.880
but these calculations are first

00:48:54.881 --> 00:48:57.550
defined without any calculation in

00:48:57.551 --> 00:48:59.995
relation to objective potentialities

00:48:59.996 --> 00:49:02.170
immediately ascribed in the present,

00:49:02.171 --> 00:49:04.075
things to do or not to do,

00:49:04.076 --> 00:49:06.265
things to say or not to say."

00:49:06.266 --> 00:49:09.880
So those immediate, those objective

00:49:09.881 --> 00:49:13.120
potentialities we can describe

00:49:13.121 --> 00:49:16.420
them mathematically using
this Affect Control Theory.

00:49:16.421 --> 00:49:19.780
What he's describing
this strategic calculation is that

00:49:19.781 --> 00:49:23.950
this flashlight beam is not just
illuminating a single action,

00:49:23.951 --> 00:49:27.040
it may be illuminating
just a couple of actions,

00:49:27.041 --> 00:49:29.755
and within the flashlight beam,

00:49:29.756 --> 00:49:32.590
I can then use my cognitive
reasoning processes

00:49:32.591 --> 00:49:34.840
in order to try to find what is

00:49:34.841 --> 00:49:36.820
the best actual action to

00:49:36.821 --> 00:49:41.660
take in order to optimize
over this utility function.

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:43.840
So this we get back to

00:49:43.841 --> 00:49:46.885
the utility function that you
were asking about earlier,

00:49:46.886 --> 00:49:50.065
I'm still going to try
and optimize this,

00:49:50.066 --> 00:49:52.210
but I'm only going to do so within

00:49:52.211 --> 00:49:55.765
this subset of
these branches of the tree.

00:49:55.766 --> 00:49:58.690
All right. So the idea
is that the humans

00:49:58.691 --> 00:50:01.375
will only do this if they have time.

00:50:01.376 --> 00:50:03.130
Alright. Time is an important thing,

00:50:03.131 --> 00:50:05.395
if time runs out I've got to act,

00:50:05.396 --> 00:50:08.905
then I'll just do the thing that's
most emotionally consistent.

00:50:08.906 --> 00:50:11.740
If I have time to think
about a little bit,

00:50:11.741 --> 00:50:13.540
then I'll wind up, maybe finding

00:50:13.541 --> 00:50:16.195
something that benefits
me a little bit more.

00:50:16.196 --> 00:50:19.540
Right. People that
do too much of this,

00:50:19.541 --> 00:50:22.285
tend to throw things into disarray,

00:50:22.286 --> 00:50:27.820
social situations,
but it goes very much

00:50:27.821 --> 00:50:30.800
in line with what Bourdieu
was talking about I believe.

