00:54:49.526 --> 00:54:51.790
then it'll still be doctor,

00:54:51.791 --> 00:54:54.805
but then it'll be
doctor on the subway.

00:54:54.806 --> 00:54:58.000
So, that additional context
I can put in as

00:54:58.001 --> 00:55:02.170
well and that context would
change things further.

00:55:02.171 --> 00:55:07.450
So, let me reiterate that
when I describe the ACP,

00:55:07.451 --> 00:55:08.740
remember we just had

00:55:08.741 --> 00:55:14.530
this three-dimensional grammar
of actors behaving on objects.

00:55:14.531 --> 00:55:17.335
So, I can augment that and
this has been done with

00:55:17.336 --> 00:55:20.920
a fourth thing which is
the setting or the context.

00:55:20.921 --> 00:55:25.435
So, I can say actor behaving
on object in some setting.

00:55:25.436 --> 00:55:29.980
So, student joking with professor in

00:55:29.981 --> 00:55:31.870
the classroom is different than

00:55:31.871 --> 00:55:34.450
student joking with
professor in the bar.

00:55:34.451 --> 00:55:35.980
&gt;&gt; In that context,

00:55:35.981 --> 00:55:41.485
what if with other [inaudible]
would it be more natural to include

00:55:41.486 --> 00:55:44.495
the same information in the MDP.

00:55:44.496 --> 00:55:47.415
I mean, because it's
changing over time.

00:55:47.416 --> 00:55:50.370
I mean, just listening
at a single, I mean,

00:55:50.371 --> 00:55:53.070
you can embed the entire state
within this context,

00:55:53.071 --> 00:55:55.495
but that seems to be much
more difficult to do

00:55:55.496 --> 00:55:58.795
than you just have the relationship.

00:55:58.796 --> 00:56:02.590
The emotional content be
able to change over time.

00:56:02.591 --> 00:56:08.425
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, the tricky thing
is that the states of the MDP,

00:56:08.426 --> 00:56:10.630
right this underlying
denotative state,

00:56:10.631 --> 00:56:14.815
this x can be very high-dimensional.

00:56:14.816 --> 00:56:18.310
This element here can
get really complicated.

00:56:18.311 --> 00:56:20.290
But the key thing is that,

00:56:20.291 --> 00:56:23.830
these pieces here
always stay the same.

00:56:23.831 --> 00:56:26.080
They're always in
this three-dimensional space and

00:56:26.081 --> 00:56:29.035
they're comparable within
that three-dimensional space.

00:56:29.036 --> 00:56:33.640
So, I don't have to model all
of this context if I can just

00:56:33.641 --> 00:56:38.185
get this emotional meaning of things.

00:56:38.186 --> 00:56:40.390
So, I can compute a whole
bunch of things just with

00:56:40.391 --> 00:56:42.250
the emotional meaning without having

00:56:42.251 --> 00:56:45.500
to figure out how the world works.

00:56:46.620 --> 00:56:47.720
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] .

00:56:47.721 --> 00:56:51.160
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, I think I'm out of time.

00:56:51.161 --> 00:56:55.615
So, I think I gave this much
more slowly than I usually do.

00:56:55.616 --> 00:56:58.610
What do you want me to do? Should I.

00:56:59.040 --> 00:57:00.140
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] .

00:57:00.141 --> 00:57:03.550
&gt;&gt; Okay. I'll skip
this prisoner's dilemma stuff,

00:57:03.551 --> 00:57:06.730
but this is a canonical,

00:57:06.731 --> 00:57:09.040
minimal working example of using

00:57:09.041 --> 00:57:11.440
this theory in practice and
I can actually show you

00:57:11.441 --> 00:57:17.450
a demo of this game play if
you'd like after the fact.

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:24.100
The other piece that I wanted to

00:57:24.101 --> 00:57:26.560
mention was the themis.cog project,

00:57:26.561 --> 00:57:28.420
is a project that's in collaboration

00:57:28.421 --> 00:57:30.250
with my colleague, Tobias Schroder,

00:57:30.251 --> 00:57:32.710
who was the guy who introduced
me the affect control theory,

00:57:32.711 --> 00:57:38.860
and he's in Germany at the Potsdam
University of Applied Sciences.

00:57:38.861 --> 00:57:41.530
Kim Rogers, who's a sociologist
at Dartmouth College,

00:57:41.531 --> 00:57:46.240
and Mei Nagappan, who's a software
engineer at Waterloo with me.

00:57:46.241 --> 00:57:48.940
So, this is funded by
this transatlantic platform,

00:57:48.941 --> 00:57:50.500
digging in a data challenge.

00:57:50.501 --> 00:57:51.910
What we look at here,

00:57:51.911 --> 00:57:55.990
is we're looking at
interactions of people

00:57:55.991 --> 00:58:00.660
on online collaborative networks
like GitHub, for example.

00:58:00.661 --> 00:58:02.685
So, that's our primary target.

00:58:02.686 --> 00:58:08.235
So, we look at people interacting
over pieces of code on GitHub,

00:58:08.236 --> 00:58:10.155
and then we're really
interested to find out

00:58:10.156 --> 00:58:12.990
how do these social and
emotional factors come into

00:58:12.991 --> 00:58:15.160
play when people are
trying to actually do

00:58:15.161 --> 00:58:18.355
something together
in a social network.

00:58:18.356 --> 00:58:21.250
So, I won't give
too many details about that.

00:58:21.251 --> 00:58:24.620
So, maybe I'll just
skip these last bits.

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:31.680
This is the affect control theory
version of the group simulator.

00:58:31.681 --> 00:58:33.420
So, I can create, instead of

00:58:33.421 --> 00:58:35.460
just doing an interaction
between two agents,

00:58:35.461 --> 00:58:38.205
I can do an interaction between
a whole group of agents.

00:58:38.206 --> 00:58:39.750
Then the blue agent is

00:58:39.751 --> 00:58:43.030
the everybody's concept
of the group as a whole,

00:58:43.031 --> 00:58:44.980
and I can use
Affect Control Theory to

00:58:44.981 --> 00:58:47.950
compute not only what people
will do to one another,

00:58:47.951 --> 00:58:49.960
but who they will do it to.

00:58:49.961 --> 00:58:52.180
The basic idea is that
people will try to

00:58:52.181 --> 00:58:54.190
interact with other people
that are going to

00:58:54.191 --> 00:58:58.435
help them confirm their sense
of self the most.

00:58:58.436 --> 00:59:02.365
So, we can do some pretty interesting
group simulations with this.

00:59:02.366 --> 00:59:05.230
Then, we've gathered
some datasets as well.

00:59:05.231 --> 00:59:06.850
So, a few datasets,

00:59:06.851 --> 00:59:09.970
so we have a new dataset of

00:59:09.971 --> 00:59:13.150
this ratings, actually
GitHub concepts.

00:59:13.151 --> 00:59:15.430
So, we're interested in
trying to find out how do

00:59:15.431 --> 00:59:18.700
developers on GitHub
rate concepts on GitHub.

00:59:18.701 --> 00:59:22.510
So, for instance, the word commit
means something different to

00:59:22.511 --> 00:59:24.220
a developer than it does to

00:59:24.221 --> 00:59:27.730
someone agreeing to
be married, let say.

00:59:27.731 --> 00:59:30.535
These were rated by
50 GitHub developers.

00:59:30.536 --> 00:59:32.380
Then, we've done some work on trying

00:59:32.381 --> 00:59:34.330
to do some sentiment analysis.

00:59:34.331 --> 00:59:37.015
So, based on poll requests
comments on GitHub,

00:59:37.016 --> 00:59:40.525
can we extract these
three-dimensional vectors from that?

00:59:40.526 --> 00:59:42.310
Then, we have a few other datasets as

00:59:42.311 --> 00:59:44.875
well that I've been working with.

00:59:44.876 --> 00:59:47.050
So, the basic idea here of

00:59:47.051 --> 00:59:49.450
this project is we're going to
have all this data coming in.

00:59:49.451 --> 00:59:53.575
We're going to use some NLP to
try to uncover what's going on,

00:59:53.576 --> 00:59:55.150
but we're going to guide that using

00:59:55.151 --> 00:59:56.860
this affect control theory and

00:59:56.861 --> 00:59:58.780
hope that the two things
meet in the middle,

00:59:58.781 --> 01:00:01.060
then that will allow us to analyze

01:00:01.061 --> 01:00:04.540
these different biases that are
present in these social networks,

01:00:04.541 --> 01:00:07.750
and possibly also allow us
to design interventions,

01:00:07.751 --> 01:00:10.000
and then artificial
agents that act in

01:00:10.001 --> 01:00:12.460
the networks in order to
help the groups become

01:00:12.461 --> 01:00:18.440
more effective or have
fewer biases, for example.

01:00:18.570 --> 01:00:20.995
So, just to sum up,

01:00:20.996 --> 01:00:22.765
a lot of Artificial Intelligence,

01:00:22.766 --> 01:00:24.760
I believe is sort of
equating intelligence

01:00:24.761 --> 01:00:27.745
with decision theoretic rationality.

01:00:27.746 --> 01:00:30.400
But we really know that
emotions are necessary for

01:00:30.401 --> 01:00:33.010
intelligence and this low road gives

01:00:33.011 --> 01:00:35.019
a heuristic social intelligence

01:00:35.020 --> 01:00:36.820
and that social
intelligence allows us to

01:00:36.821 --> 01:00:39.400
solve social dilemmas like

01:00:39.401 --> 01:00:42.200
the tragedy of
the commons, for example.

01:00:43.590 --> 01:00:47.380
Traditional AI people might say well,

01:00:47.381 --> 01:00:49.480
this is just the reason that we have

01:00:49.481 --> 01:00:52.360
these emotions as humans is
just because we're so dumb.

01:00:52.361 --> 01:00:54.100
We just need to have bigger brains

01:00:54.101 --> 01:00:55.525
and then we could do everything,

01:00:55.526 --> 01:00:58.855
decision theoretically
and we'd be all set.

01:00:58.856 --> 01:01:00.430
So, I think that that's fundamentally

01:01:00.431 --> 01:01:03.550
incorrect and one of the ways to

01:01:03.551 --> 01:01:05.350
see this is by looking at

01:01:05.351 --> 01:01:08.530
the different forms of commitment
that people make to one another.

01:01:08.531 --> 01:01:10.390
This book by Lawler describes

01:01:10.391 --> 01:01:12.625
these commitments as
being instrumental.

01:01:12.626 --> 01:01:15.505
So, these are things that
are just have to do with

01:01:15.506 --> 01:01:18.130
the practical things like if I

01:01:18.131 --> 01:01:20.770
needed to get a coffee
and I'm in a new town,

01:01:20.771 --> 01:01:26.215
that I just want to pay money to
a coffee shop and get my coffee.

01:01:26.216 --> 01:01:28.045
I don't care which coffee shop it is.

01:01:28.046 --> 01:01:29.380
If one coffee shop shuts

01:01:29.381 --> 01:01:30.895
down and okay, let's
just go to the next one.

01:01:30.896 --> 01:01:32.260
But as soon as I walk into

01:01:32.261 --> 01:01:35.815
one coffee shop and I have a little
interaction with the barista,

01:01:35.816 --> 01:01:38.110
then my instrumental commitment

01:01:38.111 --> 01:01:40.915
turns into an Affective commitment.

01:01:40.916 --> 01:01:44.110
So, now I like, "That's
my barista. I like that guy.

01:01:44.111 --> 01:01:45.700
He likes me." Next time I

01:01:45.701 --> 01:01:47.530
go to the same two coffee shops
and I'd be like,

01:01:47.531 --> 01:01:49.045
"Yeah, I want to go to that one."

01:01:49.046 --> 01:01:50.650
Then, if that one shuts down,

01:01:50.651 --> 01:01:52.420
I'm like, "Oh, no,
my heart is broken.

01:01:52.421 --> 01:01:54.730
My favorite barista is gone."

01:01:54.731 --> 01:01:57.655
These Affective commitments
are much stronger,

01:01:57.656 --> 01:02:00.505
much longer lasting,
and run much deeper.

01:02:00.506 --> 01:02:04.795
They're really what motivates humans
to interact with one another,

01:02:04.796 --> 01:02:08.755
so they strengthen the bonds
that make them longer-lasting.

01:02:08.756 --> 01:02:10.830
They also increased trust between

01:02:10.831 --> 01:02:13.290
agents and this leads
to agents being able

01:02:13.291 --> 01:02:18.315
to interact with one another
using shorter contracts.

01:02:18.316 --> 01:02:21.330
I trust this guy, so I'm
going to do what he says.

01:02:21.331 --> 01:02:24.780
That allows agent to be more
efficient and I think this

01:02:24.781 --> 01:02:28.530
is regardless of the available
cognitive resources.

01:02:28.531 --> 01:02:33.225
So, a group that interacts using
this is always going to do better

01:02:33.226 --> 01:02:39.430
than one that doesn't.
So, that's the end of it.

